Understanding exactly how the leak happens explains why `remove()` is mandatory in pooled environments.

When a thread terminates, its `ThreadLocalMap` is garbage collected along with all entries — no leak. But in a thread pool (ExecutorService, Tomcat, Spring), threads survive for the application's lifetime. The `ThreadLocalMap` grows with every unique ThreadLocal that is set without being removed.

The map uses **weak references for keys** (the ThreadLocal instances). When the ThreadLocal variable goes out of scope, the key is collected, but the **value remains** — a strong reference in the map entry with a null key. These stale entries are cleaned up lazily during subsequent set/get operations, but if no ThreadLocal operations happen on that thread, the values persist indefinitely.

```java
private static final ThreadLocal<UserContext> ctx = new ThreadLocal<>();

public void handleRequest(Request req) {
    ctx.set(new UserContext(req.getUser()));
    try {
        processRequest(req);
    } finally {
        ctx.remove(); // mandatory in pooled environments
    }
}
```

The try-finally pattern with `remove()` in the finally block is non-negotiable. Spring's `RequestContextHolder` and security frameworks like Spring Security use this pattern internally — but custom ThreadLocals in application code must follow the same discipline.

For inheritable values that propagate to child threads, `InheritableThreadLocal` copies the parent's value to child threads at creation time. But this does not work with thread pools — the pool thread is not a child of the submitting thread.
